WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.000
Episodic memory.

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.276
Facts tell an agent what you like.

3
00:00:04.276 --> 00:00:07.067
Episodes tell it what actually worked last time.

4
00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:10.360
Long-term memory stores facts.

5
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.733
Episodic memory stores experience.

6
00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:16.300
A fact is something like, the user likes lunar distances.

7
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.233
An episode is a full past run: the task, the steps taken, and the outcome.

8
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:26.500
Replayed as a worked example, it teaches the model how your environment behaves.

9
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.200
Here is how the loop works.

10
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.067
The agent runs a task.

11
00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:36.700
Afterwards we log one line per run: the task, the trace, the outcome, and the step count.

12
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.033
When a new task arrives, we embed it.

13
00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:43.567
We recall the nearest past episode and show it to the model as an example.

14
00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:46.267
Three habits make it work.

15
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:48.667
Log everything, one line per run.

16
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:53.633
Recall by similarity: embed the new task and fetch the closest episode.

17
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:55.296
And keep the failures.

18
00:00:55.296 --> 00:00:59.633
An episode that recorded a recovery teaches more than a clean success.

19
00:01:00.833 --> 00:01:02.033
Here it is in code.

20
00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:08.767
After every run we build an episode with the task, the steps, and the outcome, and append it to a log file.

21
00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:13.200
We also add the task text to the vector store, with the episode attached.

22
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.633
Before a new run, we query with the new task, take the single nearest episode, and put it into the prompt.

23
00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:22.200
Here's a real payoff.

24
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.021
On Tuesday, the DONKI service times out.

25
00:01:25.021 --> 00:01:28.867
The agent flails for six steps before using its cached table.

26
00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:33.067
That whole trajectory, including the successful fallback, is logged.

27
00:01:33.367 --> 00:01:41.433
On Friday a similar question arrives, Tuesday's episode is recalled, and the agent reaches the cache in two steps instead of six.

28
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:43.480
Let's recap.

29
00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.233
Episodic memory stores whole past runs.

30
00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:49.467
For a new task, recall the most similar episode.

31
00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:52.300
Failures that include a recovery are gold.

32
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.240
It turns experience into an example.

33
00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.367
Next up: multi-agent systems.
